# Copy this file to `.env` and set a strong, random token.
# The `.env` file is gitignored — never commit a real token.
#
# Generate a token, e.g.:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Single-user server (the default): one shared library behind one token.
# Every SnipVault app that connects must present this exact token.
SNIPVAULT_TOKEN=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Multiple users (optional): several people share one server, each with their
# OWN private vault keyed by their OWN token. Format is a comma-separated list
# of `user:token` pairs. Each user's data lives in a separate database file
# under data/users/<user>/ — isolation is structural, so one user can never see
# another's snippets. User ids may contain A–Z a–z 0–9 _ - (1–64 chars).
#
# Generate one token PER user, e.g.:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Example (uncomment and set to enable multi-user mode):
#   SNIPVAULT_TOKENS=alice:REPLACE_WITH_ALICE_TOKEN,bob:REPLACE_WITH_BOB_TOKEN
#
# If BOTH SNIPVAULT_TOKENS and SNIPVAULT_TOKEN are set, SNIPVAULT_TOKENS wins
# (multi-user) and SNIPVAULT_TOKEN is ignored. Each user just enters their own
# token in the desktop app's Settings → Sync server.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth rate limiting (optional): a sliding-window limiter on FAILED auth
# attempts, keyed by client IP, to blunt brute force on a weak token. Valid
# clients are never throttled. Defaults shown; only set these to tune them.
#   SNIPVAULT_RATELIMIT_MAX=20            # failed attempts allowed per window
#   SNIPVAULT_RATELIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000   # window length in milliseconds
